{"maxId":268,"entries":{"chapter-10-study-assessment-of-decisional-capacity-and-competencies|Chapter 10 Study: Assessment of Decisional Capacity and Competencies":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Assess understanding, appreciation, reasoning and the ability to express a choice (p151–152).":["u3"],"copy|Capacity is decision-specific; impairment in one task does not establish incapacity for every decision (p154).":["u4"],"copy|Legal incompetence is a court determination; diagnosis, age or eccentricity alone does not establish it (p152).":["u5"],"copy|A choice that seems unreasonable to others does not itself negate self-determination; assess the reasoning process (p152).":["u6"],"copy|Explain the decision clearly and address hearing, literacy, language and communication barriers (p152).":["u7"],"copy|Ask patients to describe the situation, options, consequences and their reasons in their own terms (p152).":["u8"],"copy|Immediate memory, language and frontal/executive functions support decision making (p153).":["u9"],"copy|Cognitive test scores can prompt further assessment, but no single cutoff determines capacity (p153).":["u10"],"copy|Consider pain, distress, coercion, trust, culture and the quality of communication; these may change a person's ability to participate (p153–154).":["u11"],"copy|Document the specific decision and demonstrated abilities or deficits; in many cases, a thoughtful interview is sufficient without a formal cognitive score (p154).":["u12"],"copy|Medical treatment, finances, self-care, wills and appointing a proxy involve different tasks and requirements (p154–155).":["u13"],"copy|Not every incapacitated person requires guardianship; existing powers of attorney and supportive family arrangements may suffice (p153).":["u14"],"copy|During potentially reversible incapacity, use an interim surrogate arrangement while clarifying recovery and prior wishes (p155).":["u15"],"copy|Continue involving the person even when they cannot give full informed consent; seek assent and attend to expressed wishes (p155).":["u16"],"copy|Surrogates use the person's known preferences through substituted judgment; when these cannot be established, use a best-interest approach (pp153, 155).":["u17"],"four-questions-for-one-real-decision|Four questions for one real decision":["u262"],"when-capacity-differs-between-tasks-or-over-time|When capacity differs between tasks or over time":["u263"],"frame-the-consultation-narrowly-enough-to-answer-it|Frame the consultation narrowly enough to answer it":["u264"],"taking-table-10-1-from-labels-to-evidence|Taking Table 10-1 from labels to evidence":["u265"],"cognitive-testing-communication-and-the-conditions-of-the-interview|Cognitive testing, communication and the conditions of the interview":["u266"],"assessing-treatment-refusal-and-self-care|Assessing treatment refusal and self-care":["u267"],"financial-decisions-legal-instruments-and-scope-of-protection|Financial decisions, legal instruments and scope of protection":["u268"],"fluctuating-capacity-surrogate-decisions-and-assent|Fluctuating capacity, surrogate decisions and assent":["u25"],"exam-traps|Exam traps":["u26"],"copy|Remembering the facts of a proposed treatment does not show that the person appreciates their personal implications (p152).":["u27"],"copy|A refusal, a risky choice or a preference to delegate is not itself evidence of incapacity (p151–152).":["u28"],"copy|Clinical capacity for a particular task can fluctuate, and a court's legal determination is a separate question (p152–155).":["u29"],"past-exam-questions|Past exam questions":["u30"],"copy|2024-Sep Q77 — Printed page 151.":["u31"],"recall-drill|Recall drill":["u32"],"copy|CHAPTER 10 / HAZZARD":["u19"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 10; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u20"],"copy|What capacity means":["u21"],"copy|How to assess":["u22"],"copy|Support & surrogate decisions":["u23"],"copy|Understanding asks whether the person can explain the situation, proposed intervention, alternatives and consequences in their own words.":["u38"],"copy|Appreciation goes a step further: ask what those consequences mean in this person's own circumstances.":["u39"],"copy|A patient may accurately describe the general hazards of a procedure yet deny that the illness or its consequences could apply personally (p151–152).":["u40"],"copy|Reasoning is the ability to compare alternatives and explain a choice in the light of the patient's priorities.":["u41"],"copy|A person may reasonably choose comfort over a longer life.":["u42"],"copy|Expression of choice asks for a communicated decision.":["u43"],"copy|Reliable communication can take forms other than fluent speech.":["u44"],"copy|It is possible to decline the decision-making role and voluntarily ask another person to choose (p152).":["u45"],"copy|In September 2024 Q77, memory, language and executive function are distractors because they support the assessment without replacing any of its required elements.":["u46"],"copy|Record the patient's own explanation and how the decision relates to their life, including the option of no treatment.":["u47"],"copy|A chart entry consisting only of an orientation score or a yes/no declaration provides little basis for later review (p151–154).":["u48"],"copy|A person who can decide about a treatment may make serious errors with bills.":["u49"],"copy|Assess actual performance in the relevant task and the consequences of the difficulty.":["u50"],"copy|A person unable to make a complex choice may still express stable preferences about daily care or take part in selecting a trusted decision-maker.":["u51"],"copy|Where recovery is possible, address reversible causes and revisit the specific decision (p154–155).":["u52"],"copy|A request to “assess competence” leaves the essential clinical question unanswered.":["u53"],"copy|Does the team need consent for an operation today, a plan for safe medication use at home, or help with the patient's financial affairs?":["u54"],"copy|Which alternatives are available, and does the decision need to be made now?":["u55"],"copy|A person may be capable of consenting to familiar treatment while unable to organize a complex household.":["u56"],"copy|Document the particular task, circumstances and time of assessment.":["u57"],"copy|A finding about that task should be usable by the team without implying a much broader loss of rights (p151–152, p154).":["u58"],"copy|Two obligations can pull in different directions: respecting self-determination and protecting a person who cannot make the relevant decision.":["u59"],"copy|The clinician has strong grounds to examine capacity when a choice may have grave consequences, but the seriousness of the outcome is not itself evidence that an ability is absent.":["u60"],"copy|A patient who accepts the recommendation deserves the same quality of disclosure as one who refuses.":["u61"],"copy|Conversely, an agreeable answer from a patient who cannot explain what is proposed is a weak basis for calling the consent informed (p151–152).":["u62"],"copy|Start with the patient's own account before supplying the answer.":["u63"],"copy|“What do you think is happening?”":["u64"],"copy|identifies misconceptions and the language the patient uses.":["u65"],"copy|Then explain the choices in a form the person can hear and understand.":["u66"],"copy|Avoid medical shorthand and check whether a hearing, language or literacy barrier has interfered.":["u67"],"copy|Ask again at the end of the discussion what the patient understands and expects.":["u68"],"copy|A change after clarification may indicate that the first explanation was inadequate, rather than a fundamental inability to decide (p151–152).":["u69"],"copy|Understanding includes comprehending the information, retaining it long enough for discussion and recognizing the relationship between an intervention and its possible outcome.":["u70"],"copy|It is possible to remember the name of an operation without understanding what it will change.":["u71"],"copy|Ask the patient to explain the illness, the purpose of the proposed intervention and the consequence of declining it in their own words.":["u72"],"copy|Use follow-up questions rather than rewarding familiarity with a clinical term (p152).":["u74"],"copy|Appreciation asks what those facts mean to this person.":["u75"],"copy|Imagine a patient who correctly says that untreated infection can worsen and treatment can help, but insists that the positive finding could not possibly concern them.":["u76"],"copy|Explore why.":["u77"],"copy|They may reasonably dispute an uncertain result or have misunderstood who was tested.":["u78"],"copy|Alternatively, they may be unable to recognize any connection to their own circumstances despite a clear explanation.":["u79"],"copy|The informative evidence is the patient's response to clarification, not the mere fact that the clinician and patient disagree (p151–152).":["u80"],"copy|Reasoning involves comparing alternatives and their consequences within the patient's own values.":["u81"],"copy|Ask which consequence matters most, what disadvantage the person is prepared to accept, and why one alternative fits their aims better than another.":["u82"],"copy|A person may prefer less time alive at home to more time receiving burdensome treatment.":["u83"],"copy|Another person with the same illness may accept intensive treatment for the chance of attending a family event.":["u84"],"copy|Opposite choices can both reflect intact reasoning; the evidence lies in the comparison and its connection to a coherent personal priority (p152).":["u85"],"copy|Expression of a choice is a distinct ability.":["u86"],"copy|A person may communicate a clear refusal but give no indication of understanding or appreciation.":["u87"],"copy|Conversely, expressive aphasia can make speech halting or introduce a wrong word that does not reflect what the person intends.":["u88"],"copy|Find a reliable means of communication and check that the reported answer actually represents the person's choice.":["u89"],"copy|A relative who answers before the patient has a chance to respond may make communication look easier while hiding the very ability under assessment (p152–153).":["u90"],"copy|Consider a patient who initially chooses treatment, later declines and finally asks a daughter to decide.":["u91"],"copy|These changes call for exploration, not automatic disqualification.":["u92"],"copy|Did the patient hear a new fact, forget an important consequence, change priorities after reflection, or feel pressured when the daughter arrived?":["u93"],"copy|Ask which explanation fits and see whether the choice remains intelligible in the patient's own account.":["u94"],"copy|A capable patient can deliberately delegate part or all of the decision.":["u95"],"copy|Establish that delegation is voluntary and determine how much information and participation the patient still wants (p152–154).":["u96"],"copy|Cognitive testing and observation of decision making answer related but different questions.":["u97"],"copy|A memory test tells the examiner something about retention; asking the patient to recall the options just discussed shows whether memory compromised this decision.":["u98"],"copy|A language examination may reveal receptive or expressive difficulty but does not determine whether the patient can communicate meaning by another route.":["u99"],"copy|Validate the underlying function when a response is ambiguous.":["u100"],"copy|The mapped examination item asks for the abilities demonstrated during a decision, rather than a list of neuropsychological processes that contribute to them (p151–153).":["u101"],"copy|Executive abilities can be impaired when ordinary conversation sounds intact.":["u102"],"copy|A patient may recite that there are several alternatives, yet be unable to compare their consequences, form a plan or notice an error.":["u103"],"copy|In a self-care evaluation this can appear as a plausible description of an intended routine that repeatedly fails in practice.":["u104"],"copy|Ask for an example, how the plan would be implemented and what the patient would do if it failed.":["u105"],"copy|Compare that account with demonstrated performance.":["u106"],"copy|Neither fluent conversation nor a reassuring impression during a short social visit tests the complete task (p153–154).":["u107"],"copy|A brief mental status examination can identify a reason to look more closely, prompt a more detailed evaluation or help explain a failure already observed.":["u108"],"copy|It does not yield a universally valid score below which every decision belongs to someone else.":["u109"],"copy|Someone with poor short-term memory may still understand a simple choice when it is explained and make a consistent decision.":["u110"],"copy|Someone with relatively preserved memory may have marked frontal dysfunction and no workable plan for self-care.":["u111"],"copy|Record both the cognitive finding and how it affected the decision if testing was used (p153–154).":["u112"],"copy|Consider the conditions under which the interview took place.":["u113"],"copy|Pain, anxiety, emotional distress, an unfamiliar clinician or mistrust may limit participation.":["u114"],"copy|A person may need extra time to take in information or a different explanation that fits their language and background.":["u115"],"copy|A clinician who presents a complex choice once in technical terms and then accepts silence as incapacity has not established whether the information was appropriately communicated.":["u116"],"copy|Identify a correctable barrier, address it where possible and assess the relevant abilities again (p152–154).":["u117"],"copy|Voluntariness deserves its own inquiry even when understanding, appreciation, reasoning and expression appear intact.":["u118"],"copy|Family members can help explain personal values, support communication or provide a trusted role in shared decisions.":["u119"],"copy|Their involvement may be culturally expected and freely chosen.":["u120"],"copy|It can also introduce pressure, threats or inducements.":["u121"],"copy|Ask the patient why they are making this decision and whose preferences it reflects.":["u122"],"copy|If possible, let the patient speak without the person exerting the possible influence.":["u123"],"copy|Distinguishing ordinary influence from undue pressure may require judgment about the actual circumstances (p153–154).":["u124"],"copy|For a treatment decision, provide the condition, realistic alternatives including no treatment, and the consequences sufficiently clearly for the patient to weigh them.":["u125"],"copy|In a refusal, identify the specific option declined.":["u126"],"copy|Declining an invasive procedure may coexist with acceptance of analgesia, monitoring or another intervention.":["u127"],"copy|The question “Will you follow the doctor's advice?”":["u128"],"copy|compresses these choices into obedience.":["u129"],"copy|A better interview asks the patient to describe what each option would mean, state which outcome matters and choose among the real alternatives (p152, p154).":["u130"],"copy|An illustrative note about such an interview might say that the patient explained the illness and what the procedure was intended to accomplish, identified a meaningful risk of declining it, and favored a less burdensome course because maintaining a particular ability mattered most.":["u131"],"copy|If an ability was absent, describe the finding just as specifically: for example, the patient repeatedly forgot the alternatives before any comparison could be made despite an accessible explanation.":["u132"],"copy|The chart should let a later clinician see the reasoning behind the assessment and its limits, whether or not a formal test score was obtained (p154).":["u133"],"copy|Self-care raises a different problem.":["u134"],"copy|A person may lack the physical dexterity to open medication containers but understand the risk of missed doses and accept a reliable person to prepare and check the medication.":["u135"],"copy|The task of independently organizing the pills is impaired; the ability to solve the problem by arranging appropriate help may be intact.":["u136"],"copy|A person who repeatedly claims to manage well despite demonstrated dangerous errors and cannot consider any practical support raises a different concern.":["u137"],"copy|Investigate the specific functional failure and the person's response to it before concluding that remaining at home is unsafe (p154).":["u138"],"copy|A structured conversation about home safety can begin with a concrete example such as repeated difficulty preparing a meal.":["u139"],"copy|Ask what went wrong, whether it could recur, which supports are available and whether they are feasible.":["u140"],"copy|A meal-delivery plan that the person cannot arrange or does not recognize when it arrives may not resolve the risk.":["u141"],"copy|A service the patient understands, accepts and can actually access may permit independence in other domains.":["u142"],"copy|Distinguish a choice to tolerate a recognized risk from a failure to appreciate that the risk exists (p154).":["u143"],"copy|Collateral information is useful here because self-care has an observable outcome.":["u144"],"copy|A family member may report spoiled food, missed bills or unsuccessful attempts to provide help.":["u145"],"copy|Record what was actually seen, the patient's explanation and whether the proposed intervention would address it.":["u146"],"copy|“Eccentric” tells the clinician little about planning, error detection or safety.":["u147"],"copy|Someone whose home or habits differ from the family's expectations can still understand the circumstances and make a reasoned choice.":["u148"],"copy|The strongest evidence relates the demonstrated failure to a deficit in one or more of the abilities required for the particular task (p153–154).":["u149"],"copy|Financial management can fail before other capacities.":["u150"],"copy|Repeated unpaid bills or major errors in handling money are more informative than a general impression that an older person is forgetful.":["u151"],"copy|Assess the nature and practical significance of the errors and the person's insight.":["u152"],"copy|They may recognize the problem and freely choose a reliable helper.":["u153"],"copy|Occupational therapy or neuropsychological assessment can contribute when the functional picture is uncertain.":["u154"],"copy|A conclusion that someone needs financial supervision does not, on its own, make them unable to consent to medical care or choose daily activities (p153–154).":["u155"],"copy|The chapter distinguishes judicial findings and the clinician's functional assessment in a US legal context.":["u156"],"copy|Guardianship of finances concerns money and property; guardianship of the person can encompass broader issues such as safety, care and living arrangements.":["u157"],"copy|Limited guardianship can restrict the appointed person's role to an area in which the individual cannot act.":["u158"],"copy|Previously arranged powers of attorney or an agreed, workable support arrangement sometimes make additional court involvement unnecessary.":["u159"],"copy|Where monitoring or formal authority is needed, court appointment can add oversight.":["u160"],"copy|The exact legal framework varies by jurisdiction (p152–153).":["u161"],"copy|A family relationship alone does not determine the quality of a substitute decision.":["u162"],"copy|In discussing court-appointed conservators the chapter reports family appointments in 75%–85% of cases in which a court acted.":["u163"],"copy|That proportion says nothing about whether a particular relative understands the patient's preferences, is free of conflict or can manage the practical responsibility.":["u164"],"copy|Ask what the person understood and chose before capacity was lost, what the proposed representative knows and which decision actually requires representation.":["u165"],"copy|Oversight and the scope of authority are separate from familiarity with the person (p153).":["u166"],"copy|The capacity to execute one legal document is not interchangeable with the capacity to execute all of them.":["u167"],"copy|The chapter describes the possibility that a person who cannot manage ongoing financial affairs may still understand what they wish to do with an estate and explain the logic of that choice.":["u168"],"copy|Similarly, someone may identify a person they trust to make health decisions and explain why, even when the details of a complex treatment are beyond them.":["u169"],"copy|Document the task the interview addressed.":["u170"],"copy|A decision about appointing an agent is not a retrospective declaration that all earlier documents are valid or invalid (p154–155).":["u171"],"copy|A living will can pose a different challenge: it asks the person to anticipate clinical conditions they have never experienced and imagine the consequences of treatments in those settings.":["u172"],"copy|Even people with intact cognition may find that abstraction difficult.":["u173"],"copy|Clarify terms and explore the person's values and examples, rather than assuming that a signature demonstrates understanding of every hypothetical future event.":["u174"],"copy|Preferences expressed about concrete experiences can remain informative when a person later has difficulty discussing a broad hypothetical scenario (p155).":["u175"],"copy|A referral letter about finances, for example, should say which records or tasks were reviewed, who provided the observations, what errors actually occurred and how the individual addressed them.":["u176"],"copy|If the person accurately identifies a trusted agent but cannot describe the estate or the consequences of a particular transaction, report those findings separately.":["u177"],"copy|The assessor need not turn an answer about one document into an opinion on every aspect of personal and financial life.":["u178"],"copy|This preserves the person's remaining abilities while giving the decision maker specific information about the problem that prompted the referral (p153–155).":["u179"],"copy|Delirium or a recent stroke can impair decision making while leaving recovery uncertain.":["u180"],"copy|Determine whether the decision can wait and what harm might arise from delay.":["u181"],"copy|If it can wait, address reversible barriers and reassess when participation is more likely.":["u182"],"copy|If it cannot, identify an appropriate interim decision maker and look for previously expressed wishes.":["u183"],"copy|The temporary need for a surrogate is not a declaration of permanent global incapacity.":["u184"],"copy|Record who will review the issue and restore the patient's role as abilities return (p155).":["u185"],"copy|The chapter gives special weight to earlier wishes when an interim decision must be made.":["u186"],"copy|If those wishes are unknown and recovery remains possible, it discusses protecting life or continued function until the patient can participate again or the impairment is better understood.":["u187"],"copy|This is a response to uncertainty in the specific situation described.":["u188"],"copy|A reliable, relevant prior refusal would change the reasoning, as would new information about prognosis.":["u189"],"copy|Keep the factual uncertainty, the patient's values and the decision-maker's authority distinct in the documentation (p155).":["u190"],"copy|Substituted judgment asks what this individual would choose based on earlier statements and long-held beliefs.":["u191"],"copy|Best-interest reasoning becomes relevant when preferences cannot be established.":["u192"],"copy|A surrogate's statement “I would want this treatment” answers a different question from “She repeatedly said she would accept this burden to achieve that outcome.”":["u193"],"copy|Ask for the experiences or statements that support the latter account.":["u194"],"copy|Continue observing present preferences and signs of comfort, distress or willingness to cooperate even if those observations cannot substitute for informed consent to the entire plan (p153, p155).":["u195"],"copy|Research adds uncertainty beyond a familiar therapeutic choice.":["u196"],"copy|The participant may need to understand that the personal benefit is uncertain and that a study has purposes beyond choosing what is best for this one patient.":["u197"],"copy|Ask the person or surrogate why participation is proposed and assess voluntariness.":["u198"],"copy|The chapter discusses combining surrogate consent with the patient's assent where appropriate.":["u199"],"copy|A person who cannot grasp the full protocol can still indicate whether the procedures cause pain or whether they wish to cooperate.":["u200"],"copy|That information matters especially when participation requires the patient's active cooperation (p155).":["u201"],"copy|1.":["u202"],"copy|Which four abilities are assessed in the September 2024 item?":["u203"],"copy|2.":["u204"],"copy|Which question tests understanding?":["u205"],"copy|3.":["u206"],"copy|How do you move from understanding to appreciation?":["u207"],"copy|4.":["u208"],"copy|How can a patient show reasoning while declining the clinician's recommendation?":["u209"],"copy|5.":["u210"],"copy|What must the assessment establish about the patient's choice?":["u211"],"copy|6.":["u212"],"copy|Why clarify hearing and language before reaching a conclusion?":["u213"],"copy|7.":["u214"],"copy|Where do memory, language and frontal executive functions fit?":["u215"],"copy|8.":["u216"],"copy|Can a screening score establish capacity for a particular decision?":["u217"],"copy|9.":["u218"],"copy|Why ask a patient why they are making a choice in private when appropriate?":["u219"],"copy|10.":["u220"],"copy|Does inability to manage bills establish incapacity to consent to medical treatment?":["u221"],"copy|11.":["u222"],"copy|What belongs in documentation of a treatment decision?":["u223"],"copy|12.":["u224"],"copy|What arrangement can suffice when financial capacity is impaired?":["u225"],"copy|13.":["u226"],"copy|How should an uncertain period of incapacity be approached?":["u227"],"copy|14.":["u228"],"copy|How do substituted judgment and best interests differ?":["u229"],"copy|15.":["u230"],"copy|How can an individual participate even if unable to give full consent?":["u231"],"copy|Answer":["u232","u234","u236","u238","u240","u242","u244","u246","u248","u250","u252","u254","u256","u258","u260"],"copy|Understanding, appreciation, reasoning and expressing a choice (p151–152).":["u233"],"copy|Ask the patient to explain the condition, available options and likely consequences in their own words (p152).":["u235"],"copy|Ask how the described risks, benefits and outcomes would affect this individual personally (p152).":["u237"],"copy|Compare the options and explain how their consequences fit a consistent personal or religious priority (p152).":["u239"],"copy|That the patient can communicate a decision. A person with capacity may also choose to delegate decision making (p152).":["u241"],"copy|A failure of communication can look like poor understanding. Present the information so the patient can access it and then reassess (p152).":["u243"],"copy|They enable comprehension, retention, comparison and communication. They are underlying abilities rather than substitutes for the four criteria (p151–153).":["u245"],"copy|No cutoff settles the question. Evaluate the abilities needed for the actual decision under consideration (p153–154).":["u247"],"copy|The answer may clarify their values or reveal coercion or undue influence. Interpretation depends on the circumstances (p153–154).":["u249"],"copy|No. These require different abilities. Look for observed financial errors and examine capacity for the treatment separately (p154).":["u251"],"copy|The options including no treatment, the patient's account of potential outcomes, how they apply personally, the reasons and the expressed choice (p154).":["u253"],"copy|A previously authorized power of attorney or appropriate assistance may make formal guardianship unnecessary, depending on circumstances (p153–154).":["u255"],"copy|Use interim decision support and an appropriate surrogate while treating reversible problems and reassessing potential recovery (p155).":["u257"],"copy|Substituted judgment follows what the person would have chosen. Best interests guides decisions when those preferences cannot be established (p153, p155).":["u259"],"copy|Seek assent and attend to preferences or distress, alongside the surrogate's informed decision; cooperation may be essential to the intervention (p155).":["u261"],"copy|Repeating the consent form word for word can conceal an inability to make that link.":["u73"]}}
